# Lecture 11D

## UPGMA: Five Sequences and Weighted Averages

## Chapter 11

## Dr. Neil Voss

## November 17, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Build a UPGMA tree from a distance matrix.
- Update distances using every gene in each cluster.
- Calculate node heights and branch lengths.
- Explain what the completed tree does and does not preserve.

=== layout: section

# Build a Gene Tree

=== layout: one-panel

# Our Five-Gene Example

@body

- These example distances are not biological measurements or years.
- A smaller distance means a closer pair in this example.
- Read one half of the matrix: each pair appears once.
- We will build the tree using UPGMA, a distance-based clustering method.

=== layout: subsection

# Plan the Construction

=== layout: one-panel

# The Procedure

@body

1. Read the matrix and find the smallest distance.
2. Merge the closest clusters; node height = distance / 2.
3. Update all distances from the new cluster.
4. Calculate branch lengths and check the tree.
5. Repeat until all genes have joined. DONE!

=== layout: one-panel

# Keep Track of Cluster Members

@body

- At first, a, b, c, d, and e are single-gene clusters.
- For example, a hypothetical xy cluster contains both x and y.
- To update a distance, average the original distances between all gene pairs across the two clusters.
- Keep the original matrix and cluster members to find all the pairs.

=== layout: one-panel

# Node Height or Branch Length?

@body

- The merge distance divided by 2 gives the height of the new node above the tips.
- A branch runs from a child node to its parent node.
- Branch length = parent height - child height; a gene tip has height 0.
- Find the new height first. Subtract each child's height to label its branch.

=== layout: subsection

# Work the Example

=== include: gene_tree_five_steps.djot

=== layout: one-panel

# Read the Finished Tree

@body

- Every tip is 11 distance units from the root: UPGMA constructs an ultrametric tree.
- For a and b, the tree path is 11 + 11 = 22; the input distance is 18.
- The tree fits cluster averages. Individual input distances may not match tree paths.
- For a, b, and c, the input distances are 18, 20, and 4. The two largest differ, so the input is not ultrametric.

=== layout: one-panel

# What Does the Clock Assume?

@body

- UPGMA assumes a constant rate across lineages when interpreting tree height as time.
- Different rates, selection, and random change can make that assumption a poor fit.
- A tree does not prove a constant molecular clock or the true history.

=== layout: one-panel

# UPGMA Reference

@body

1. Start each gene as its own cluster at height 0.
2. Find the smallest current distance; join those clusters at half that distance.
3. Average all original between-cluster gene pairs to update every new distance.
4. Label each new branch: parent height minus child height.
5. Repeat until one cluster remains; check tip heights and interpretation.

=== layout: one-panel

# Other Ways to Build Trees

@body

- Neighbor joining uses a distance matrix but does not require equal tip-to-root distances.
- Maximum likelihood and Bayesian methods evaluate trees using models of sequence evolution.
- The method and its assumptions affect how we read the tree.

=== layout: bookend

# Summary

@body

- Join the closest clusters. New node height = distance / 2.
- Average all original gene pairs between the two clusters.
- Branch length = parent height - child height.
- All root-to-tip paths are equal. Some tree distances differ from the original matrix.
- UPGMA assumes the same rate of change across lineages.

=== layout: one-panel

# References

@body

- Sokal RR and Michener CD (1958). A statistical method for evaluating systematic relationships. University of Kansas Science Bulletin 38: 1409-1438.
- Felsenstein J (2004). Inferring Phylogenies. Sinauer Associates.

